{{redirect|Generació Unitat|the television series|Generació Unitat (TV series)}}

The '''unification generation''' ({{lang|ca|Generació Unitat}}) is a demographic cohort in [[Balboa]] comprising individuals born between approximately 1968 and 1982 — the first generation to grow up entirely in a reunified Balboan state following the [[Treaty of Areza|1973 reunification]]. The term derives from the [[Generació Unitat (TV series)|television drama series]] of the same name, broadcast by [[BalboaVisió]] from 1999, which depicted the lives of a group of friends from different ethnic and linguistic backgrounds in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and was explicitly targeted at this cohort's experience of a multicultural, post-reunification urban environment. The label was subsequently adopted in Balboan sociological and cultural commentary to describe the generation whose formative years coincided with the [[Gilded age of Balboan cinema|Gilded Age]] (1973–1994) and whose coming of age was shaped by the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis|asset crisis]], the [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola|El Desig trial]], and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]].

== Historical context ==

Members of the unification generation were born after or shortly before the [[Treaty of Areza]] of 1 July 1973, which transferred the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor mandate]] and the [[Balboa Canal]] zone back to Balboan sovereignty after seventy-four years of foreign control. The oldest members, born in 1968, were five years old at reunification and have no personal memory of the pre-reunification division between the mandate territory and the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]]. The youngest, born in 1982, were born nine years after reunification and have no lived experience of the transitional period at all.

The generation grew up during the [[1968–1978 Balboa recession]], the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of 1978–1987, and the subsequent asset crisis of 1985–1991. Their childhood and adolescence coincided with the deindustrialisation of [[Sant Cristòfor]] — the closure of the [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]] steel mill in 1982 and the chemical plant in 1987 — and the racial integration struggles that followed the enactment of the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The [[Siege of Murado Ranch]] (1974), the [[Sant Bart University bombing]] (1975), the [[La Guàrdia Campaign]] (1975–1976), the [[Portblanc]] school desegregation crisis (1982–1984), and the [[Hill Wars]] of the 1980s all occurred during their childhood and adolescence.

== Cultural formation ==

The unification generation's cultural references were shaped by the [[Gilded age of Balboan cinema|Gilded Age of Balboan cinema]]. The satirical films of [[Jordi Molins i Castell]] — ''[[El Palau (film)|El Palau]]'' (1979), ''[[Els Especuladors (film)|Els Especuladors]]'' (1983), and ''[[L'Últim Sopar (film)|L'Últim Sopar]]'' (1986) — and the popular comedies of [[Pere Bosch i Vargas]] — ''[[L'Inquilí (film)|L'Inquilí]]'' (1980), ''[[El Veí (film)|El Veí]]'' (1982), and ''[[La Mudança (film)|La Mudança]]'' (1985) — were the mainstream cultural products of their childhood and adolescence. The underground films of [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]], [[Kwame Rovira]], and other feminist, LGBT, and Black Balboan subcultural filmmakers circulated through community centres and informal networks that were accessible to older members of the generation.

The [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola|El Desig trial]] of 1988 was a formative cultural moment for the generation. Members of the unification generation were between six and twenty years old at the time of the trial, and the outcome — the acquittal of [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]] and the effective gutting of the [[Public Decency Act]] — established the parameters of artistic freedom under which they would come of age. The trial's galvanisation of feminist, LGBT, and civil liberties movements coincided with the generation's adolescence and shaped their political consciousness.

== Characteristics ==

The unification generation is distinguished from the preceding reunification generation — born in the 1950s and early 1960s, who experienced the reunification as a conscious political event — by the absence of any personal memory of the pre-reunification division. For the unification generation, the [[Twelve Guarantees]], the multilingual urban environment of [[Sant Cristòfor]], and the institutional framework of the reunified state were features of the landscape they grew up in rather than achievements they had witnessed. Sociological commentary on the generation has noted its tendency to regard the Guarantees as a baseline condition rather than a contested political settlement, and its impatience with older generations' attachment to the political divisions of the mandate and rump-state periods.

The generation grew up in the multilingual environment of the canal corridor, where [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Balboan Creole]], and [[Zong language|Zong]] coexisted in daily use. Code-switching between Volisanian and Creole was a normal feature of urban life for the generation, and the Fosian community's cultural institutions — Tianist temples, Zong-language schools, community organisations — were part of the urban landscape rather than exotic additions to it. The generation's experience of multiculturalism as a default condition, rather than as a policy or an achievement, has been identified as a defining characteristic in Balboan sociological literature.

The generation's economic outlook was shaped by the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] and its collapse. Members of the generation witnessed the inflation of property values during their childhood, the [[Banc Balboa]] scandal and the arrest of [[Tân Hông-un]] in 1991, and the acquisition of Banc Balboa by the Volisanian conglomerate [[Grup Financer Central]] in 1992. The [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]], which produced a GDP contraction of 4.2 per cent and unemployment of 14.8 per cent, occurred as the oldest members of the generation were entering the labour market. The experience of economic turbulence during their formative years has been associated with a scepticism toward speculative wealth and a suspicion of economic booms that distinguishes the generation from both the preceding reunification generation and the subsequent post-recession cohort.

The generation's relationship with the [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP) was shaped by the party's internal divisions during their childhood. The conflict between the leftist faction of [[Miquel Torrent i Alvarado]] and the centre-right faction of [[Roser Muntaner i Ferrer]], the emergence of the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB) in the 1980s, and the corruption scandals associated with the real-estate bubble produced a political disillusionment within the generation that has been documented in post-reunification political analysis. The generation's political behaviour has been characterised by lower partisan attachment to the BPP than the reunification generation and a greater willingness to support the FSB, the [[Liberal Party (Balboa)|Liberal Party]], or independent candidates.

== The Generació Unitat television series ==

{{main|Generació Unitat (TV series)}}

The television drama series ''[[Generació Unitat (TV series)|Generació Unitat]]'', broadcast by [[BalboaVisió]] from 1999, was the cultural product most closely associated with the generation. The series followed a group of friends from different ethnic and linguistic backgrounds living in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and depicted their navigation of the multicultural urban environment that the generation had grown up in. The series' title — adopted by the network as a label for the generation it was targeting — entered broader Balboan cultural usage as the accepted name for the cohort. The founding of [[BalboaVisió]] in 1998, explicitly oriented toward the unification generation's experience, was itself a product of the generation's arrival at cultural and commercial prominence.

== Adulthood ==

By 2026, members of the unification generation are between 44 and 58 years old and occupy positions of cultural, commercial, and political influence in Balboa. The generation constitutes much of the professional establishment of the canal corridor economy, the media industry, and the cultural institutions of [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The founders and early leadership of [[BalboaVisió]] — including [[Jordi Calvet i Mas]] (born 1969) and [[Núria Prat i Bosch]] (born 1971) — are members of the generation or its immediate predecessor.

The generation's transition to adulthood coincided with the establishment of the [[BTPMEU|Balboa–Tapuya–Potocsí–Merica Economic Union]] (BTPMEU) in 1998 and the subsequent regional integration of the Balboan economy. Members of the generation were the first to enter the labour market in the post-recession, post-BTPMEU environment, and their professional careers have been shaped by the regional integration of trade, the rise of digital media, and the transformation of the Sant Cristòfor urban economy from the deindustrialised landscape of their childhood into the services-and-logistics economy of the 2000s and 2010s.

The [[Adonian pandemic|Adonian pandemic of 2020]] occurred when the generation was in its late thirties to early fifties, and the shift toward streaming media and remote work that followed the pandemic reinforced the generation's position as the bridge between the analog cultural world of the Gilded Age and the digital environment of the post-pandemic period.

